#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199153} :activity/id #uuid "d0314b3c-4fcd-5edc-90d4-c578aa6610e7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050558 :topic/id #uuid "537a9014-df39-5285-bcbe-727d2aec6910" :topic/math-academy-id 4433} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199581 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $a$ and $b$ are integers where $a \\neq 0,$ the notation \n\n$$\na \\mid b\n$$\n\nmeans that $a$ divides $b.$ This means there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\nb = a \\cdot k.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, \n$a \\not\\mid b$\ndenotes the fact that $a$ does not divide $b.$\n\nThis simple definition can be used to prove some basic properties of integer divisibility." :tutorial/id #uuid "3429ab2c-04b0-5450-b0d5-9f615df712b7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8334 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "377a268d-59f7-5f3e-a08b-06c2d601fc8e" :step/math-academy-id 160284 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199586 :tutorial/content "The first property we'll study is called the **transitivity of divisibility.** It states the following:\n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ with $a, b\\neq 0,$ we have*\n\n$\\qquad$ $a \\mid b \\quad$ *and* $\\quad b \\mid c$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $a \\mid c.$\n\nWe can prove this statement using the definition of divisibility.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $a \\mid b,$ there exists an integer $k_1$ such that*\n\n$$\nb=ak_1.\n$$\n\n*Similarly, given that $b \\mid c,$ there exists an integer $k_2$ such that*\n\n$$\nc=bk_2.\n$$\n\n*Substituting the expression for $b$ into our equation for $c,$ we get*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =b{k}_{2} \\\\  & =(a{k}_{1}){k}_{2} \\\\  & =a({k}_{1}{k}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Since $k_1$ and $k_2$ are integers, their product $k=k_1k_2$ is also an integer. Therefore,* \n\n$$\nc = ak\n$$\n\n*which, by the definition of divisibility, means that $a \\mid c.$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1a702e8-65dc-5088-a2f3-3c2f8a836763" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8341 :tutorial/title "Transitivity of Divisibility"} :step/id #uuid "15e2f8f5-13e7-5747-b4e2-86d476512e38" :step/math-academy-id 160285 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198309 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198207 :question/id #uuid "e16c9c14-7461-51b3-b16a-e62e76527719" :question/math-academy-id "e-13322"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb430d5-5241-56a2-96be-73f44b0f2114" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048604}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420268}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Transitivity of Divisibility"} :step/id #uuid "7b57670d-d6c4-51c4-87b5-d2e4a69928b2" :step/math-academy-id 160286 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199156}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199582 :tutorial/content "Let's now state our next property, known as the **divisibility of a product:** \n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$*\n\n$\\qquad$ $c \\mid ab \\quad$ *and* $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $c \\mid b$\n\nThis statement assumes that $\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1$ (i.e., that $a$ and $c$ are coprime). This suggests we may want to use *Bézout’s identity* to prove this statement since it relates two integers to their greatest common divisor. \n\nRecall that Bézout’s identity states that for any two integers $a$ and $b,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that \n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$ is the greatest common divisor of $a$ and $b.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's prove this statement.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,c)=1,$ by Bézout’s identity there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n\n$$\nau + cv = 1.\n$$\n\n*On the other hand, since $c\\: | \\: ab,$ there exists an integer $k$ such that*\n\n$$\nab=ck.\n$$\n\n*Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $b$ and substituting $ab=ck,$ we get the following:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+cv & =1 \\\\ (au+cv)b & =b \\\\ (ab)u+cvb & =b \\\\ (ck)u+cvb & =b \\\\ c(ku+vb) & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*As $k,u,v,$ and $b$ are integers, then $ku+ vb = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n\n$$\ncm = b\n$$\n\n*which implies that*\n\n$$\nc\\: | \\: b.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "121be285-71f2-5cad-b61b-7b60b57133de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8336 :tutorial/title "The Divisibility of a Product"} :step/id #uuid "70ef53db-ac25-5e22-addb-0d6434cdf48a" :step/math-academy-id 160289 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198310 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198202 :question/id #uuid "c3086c7e-3018-5071-a7ad-cc49f139685e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13317"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b3101aa-ee9e-5940-b170-2f4e78802150" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198889} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420269}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Divisibility of a Product"} :step/id #uuid "5edb6723-f7fe-5f31-9be5-558c39b42601" :step/math-academy-id 160290 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199583 :tutorial/content "Let's now state our final property, known as the **divisibility of an integer by a product:**\n\n**Theorem**\n\n*For any integers $a,$ $b,$ and $c,$*\n\n$\\qquad$ *$a \\:|\\: c,$ $\\quad b \\:|\\: c, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ $\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad$ $ab \\:|\\: c.$*\n\nThe proof is given below.\n\n**Proof**\n\n*Given that $\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1,$ there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that*\n\n$$\nau + bv = 1.\n$$\n\n*On the other hand, since $a\\: | \\: c$ and $b\\: | \\: c,$ there exist integers $k$ and $l$ such that*\n\n$$\nc=ak,\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nc=bl.\n$$\n\n*Multiplying both sides of the first equation by $c$ and substituting $c=ak$ and $c=bl,$ we get the following:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}au+bv & =1 \\\\ (au+bv)c & =c \\\\ auc+bvc & =c \\\\ au(bl)+bv(ak) & =c \\\\ ab(ul+vk) & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*As $k,l,u,$ and $v$ are integers, then $ul + vk = m$ is an integer too. Hence,*\n\n$$\nab\\cdot m = c\n$$\n\n*which implies that*\n\n$$\nab\\: | \\: c.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dfd70186-b481-56fe-81a7-295c945277da" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8337 :tutorial/title "Divisibility by a Product"} :step/id #uuid "75e79121-3a20-5e32-ac9b-1807142f2405" :step/math-academy-id 160293 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198203 :question/id #uuid "5faa797e-0bc2-53b3-875e-6d26b82d7074" :question/math-academy-id "e-13318"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c37bf66e-a574-5cf1-9c1a-fdfda98ca27b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420266}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Divisibility by a Product"} :step/id #uuid "b53ecb10-21ac-5b8b-8577-b8b6831907c9" :step/math-academy-id 160294}] :activity/title "Properties of Integer Divisibility" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198548}]}
